  "reason": "Evidence: config_005 (rna_only, multiprofile, scvi_n200, avgpool) achieved the best result so far (acc=0.650, F1=0.628), a large jump over its singleprofile counterpart config_004 (acc=0.520). This strongly indicates multiprofile aggregation is the most impactful design axis. However, the aggregation method itself is untested beyond avgpool—config_005 is the only multiprofile experiment completed. Question: Does transformer aggregation (config_006) outperform simple avgpool for combining multiple scvi_n200 profiles? This is a clean single-variable comparison (only aggregation changes from config_005), directly isolating whether learning inter-profile interactions adds value over uniform averaging. Why best next: It exploits the strongest promising direction while reducing uncertainty about a key architectural choice (aggregation) before committing budget to more complex multimodal or alternative-encoder configurations. If transformer helps, it justifies further investment in sophisticated aggregation; if not, avgpool suffices and budget can shift to testing scgpt or multimodal fusion."
